bg.2018.2.6.reviewed_statement:
On a planet there are $M$ countries and $N$ cities. Two-way roads have been built between some of the cities. It is known that:

(1) every country has at least three cities;

(2) from every city in any country there are roads to at least half of the remaining cities in that country;

(3) from every city there is exactly one road to a city in some other country;

(4) between the cities of two different countries there are at most two roads;

(5) if two countries have together fewer than $2M$ cities, then there is at least one road between these two countries.

Prove that a traveller can visit at least $M + \frac{N}{2}$ cities, travelling only along the roads, and finally return to the city from which he started.

it.2013.0.1.reviewed_statement:
A model car is tested on some closed circuits $600$ metres long, made up of flat stretches and of uphill or downhill stretches. All uphill and downhill stretches have the same slope. The tests reveal some curious facts:

(a) the speed of the car depends only on whether it is going uphill, on the flat or downhill; calling these three speeds $v_s$, $v_p$, $v_d$ respectively, we have $v_s < v_p < v_d$;

(b) $v_s$, $v_p$ and $v_d$, expressed in metres per second, are integers;

(c) however the circuit is made up (with more or fewer climbs and descents), the time needed to complete it is always $50$ seconds.

Find all possible values of $v_s$, $v_p$, $v_d$.

it.2008.0.1.reviewed_statement:
Let $ABCDEFGHILMN$ be a regular dodecagon. Let $P$ be the intersection of the diagonals $AF$ and $DH$. Let $S$ be the circle through $A$ and $H$ that is congruent to the circumcircle of the dodecagon and distinct from it. Prove that:

(a) $P$ lies on $S$;

(b) the center of $S$ lies on the diagonal $HN$;

(c) the length of $PE$ equals the side of the dodecagon.

ee.2004.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $n$ and $c$ be coprime positive integers. For any integer $i$, let $i'$ denote the remainder of $ci$ upon division by $n$. Let $A_0A_1 \ldots A_{n-1}$ be a regular $n$-gon. Prove that

a) if the segments $A_iA_j$ and $A_kA_l$ are parallel, then the segments $A_{i'}A_{j'}$ and $A_{k'}A_{l'}$ are also parallel;

b) if the segments $A_iA_j$ and $A_kA_l$ are perpendicular, then the segments $A_{i'}A_{j'}$ and $A_{k'}A_{l'}$ are also perpendicular.

frt.2019.4.10.reviewed_statement:
Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a circle of radius $1$ and let $T$ be a real number. A set of triangles is called $T$-daring if it satisfies the following three conditions:
- the vertices of each triangle lie on $\mathcal{C}$;
- the triangles have pairwise disjoint interiors (but two triangles may share a side or a vertex);
- each triangle has perimeter strictly greater than $T$.
Find all real numbers $T$ such that, for every integer $n\ge 1$, there exists a $T$-daring set containing exactly $n$ triangles.

hkt.2014.1.5.reviewed_statement:
Find the largest integer $n$ satisfying the following conditions:

(i) $n^2$ can be expressed as the difference of two consecutive cubes;

(ii) $2n + 79$ is a perfect square.
